    "passage": "For most of the twentieth century, ecologists assumed that tropical coral reefs owed their extraordinary species diversity to long-term environmental stability. Because reef-building corals require warm, clear, shallow water within a narrow temperature range, it seemed logical that ecosystems persisting under such constant conditions would accumulate species gradually over geological time without the periodic disturbances that reset succession in temperate habitats. This stability hypothesis predicted that reefs with the longest uninterrupted histories would harbor the greatest numbers of species.\n\nSubsequent research has complicated this picture considerably. Beginning in the 1980s, studies of Caribbean and Indo-Pacific reefs showed that moderate levels of physical disturbance—storms, episodes of freshwater dilution, brief temperature anomalies—actually maintain high diversity by preventing any single coral species from monopolizing substrate. Joseph Connell, whose work on Queensland's Great Barrier Reef became widely cited, proposed what came to be called the intermediate disturbance hypothesis: diversity peaks not at the extremes of disturbance frequency but at intermediate levels, because frequent disturbance eliminates sensitive specialists while complete calm allows competitive dominants to exclude inferior competitors. The hypothesis was appealing partly because it reframed disturbance as a structuring force rather than a merely destructive one.\n\nHowever, researchers studying reef resilience in the 1990s identified a critical asymmetry that the intermediate disturbance hypothesis had not fully addressed. Recovery from disturbance depends on larval supply—the availability of free-swimming coral larvae to recolonize bare substrate—and larval supply is itself sensitive to the spatial configuration of surviving reef patches. When disturbance is spatially extensive, as in the bleaching events that accompany El Niño–driven warming, surviving donor reefs may be too few or too distant to replenish denuded areas within the window that bare substrate remains colonizable before algae overgrow it. Under these conditions, a disturbance intensity that would be "intermediate" by historical standards can trigger a shift to an algae-dominated state from which recovery is extremely slow. This threshold behavior suggests that diversity on reefs is not a simple function of disturbance frequency but also of the spatial structure of the reef network through which larvae must travel.\n\nThe picture that has emerged, then, is one in which reef diversity reflects the interaction of at least three factors: disturbance regime, competitive dynamics among coral species, and larval connectivity across the broader reef seascape. A key limitation of current models is that they treat larval connectivity as a static background condition rather than as a variable that is itself altered by successive disturbances. If each major bleaching event reduces the number and biomass of spawning adults, the larval supply available for subsequent recovery may decline in ways that compound over time, making reefs progressively less resilient even if the physical disturbance regime does not intensify.",
